<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">1556 Shaanxi earthquake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1556">1556</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jiajing Great Earthquake</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1556 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> earthquake, one of the deadliest earthquakes in history, struck Huaxian in Shaanxi in the early morning of 23 January 1556, during the reign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiajing-emperor">Jiajing Emperor</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. Most people there lived in yaodongs, artificial caves in loess cliffs, which collapsed on those sleeping inside, and more than 97 counties in ten provinces were affected. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> Earthquake Administration puts the direct deaths at roughly 100,000, while 730,000 more migrated away or died of famine and plague, a total fall of 830,000 in the registered population. Modern estimates of its magnitude range from about Mw 8.0 down to 7.0–7.5.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Huaxian lies within the Weihe Basin, one of the rift basins that form the southern and eastern boundaries of the Ordos Block and join the Shanxi Rift System to the east. The basins of the Weihe-Shanxi Rift System are bounded by large normal faults, which have been responsible for large historical earthquakes, and the Weihe Basin has an overall half-graben geometry, with the Huashan Fault and the North Qinling Fault forming its southern boundary. Millions of people in the area lived in yaodongs, artificial caves cut into the loess cliffs of the Loess Plateau, and this was a major contributing factor to the very high death toll.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The epicentre lay in the Wei River Valley near Huaxian, Weinan and Huayin. Huaxian was completely destroyed, with more than half its residents killed, and the situation in Weinan and Huayin was similar. In places crevices 20 metres deep opened in the ground, and landslides added to the deaths. Destruction reached as far as 500 kilometres from the epicentre, and more than 97 counties in the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a> and Anhui were affected.</p><p>A local account in the annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> says that mountains and rivers changed places, roads were destroyed, and in some places the ground rose to form new hills or sank to form new valleys. Of the 114 Kaicheng Stone Classics in the Forest of Stone, 40 were broken, and the shaking reduced the Small Wild Goose Pagoda in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a> by three levels. The scholar Qin Keda, who lived through it, advised that people indoors at the start of a quake should not go out at once but crouch down and wait.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Publications of the China Earthquake Administration put the direct deaths at roughly 100,000, while 730,000 more migrated or died of famine and plague, a reduction of 830,000 in imperial hukou registration. Because the Administration did not estimate total deaths, a few of its staff scientists gave their own figures, such as 450,000 or 530,000. Studies of scarps along the Huashan and Weinan faults suggest the rupture ran about 90 km, and estimates of the magnitude differ between studies. In Chinese records the event is called the Jiajing Great Earthquake, after the reigning emperor.</p><p>The Portuguese Dominican friar Gaspar da Cruz heard of it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> later in 1556 and reported it in his Treatise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> of 1569, seeing it as a possible punishment for people's sins and the Great Comet of 1556 as a possible sign of it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1556 Shaanxi earthquake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
